{"id": "29f0310d-fad8-446d-98cc-cc460138536e", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T10:59:24.001136", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "The FsLexYacc 11.x tool (which is based on ocamllex/FsLex) has a different syntax for defining character class macros. In newer versions, the `let` bindings for character classes need to be defined differently. \n\nLooking at the FsLexYacc 11.x documentation and source, the issue is that in version 11.x, the lexer file format changed. The `let` bindings for character classes are no longer supported in the same way. Instead, we should use inline character class definitions directly in the rules.\n\nLet me try a different approach - define the character classes inline without using `let` bindings, or use the `%{ ... %}` header block properly.\n\nActually, looking more carefully at FsLexYacc 11.x, the issue might be that the `let` bindings need to be in a separate section. In the newer FsLex format, the structure is:\n1. Header in `{ }`\n2. Character class definitions (using `let` or `%{` syntax)\n3. Rules\n\nBut in version 11.x, the `let` bindings for character classes were removed. We need to use inline character classes directly.\n\nLet me rewrite the lexer to use inline character classes.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "7c566d27-445f-42bd-89aa-ab8334651d86", "tool_call": {"id": "7c566d27-445f-42bd-89aa-ab8334651d86", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The FsLexYacc 11.x tool (which is based on ocamllex/FsLex) has a different syntax for defining character class macros. In newer versions, the `let` bindings for character classes need to be defined differently. \\n\\nLooking at the FsLexYacc 11.x documentation and source, the issue is that in version 11.x, the lexer file format changed. The `let` bindings for character classes are no longer supported in the same way. Instead, we should use inline character class definitions directly in the rules.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach - define the character classes inline without using `let` bindings, or use the `%{ ... %}` header block properly.\\n\\nActually, looking more carefully at FsLexYacc 11.x, the issue might be that the `let` bindings need to be in a separate section. In the newer FsLex format, the structure is:\\n1. Header in `{ }`\\n2. Character class definitions (using `let` or `%{` syntax)\\n3. Rules\\n\\nBut in version 11.x, the `let` bindings for character classes were removed. We need to use inline character classes directly.\\n\\nLet me rewrite the lexer to use inline character classes.\\n\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-62d5e8d2-b37f-4689-ba6c-450c11c6dce2", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"The FsLexYacc 11.x tool (which is based on ocamllex/FsLex) has a different syntax for defining character class macros. In newer versions, the `let` bindings for character classes need to be defined differently. \\n\\nLooking at the FsLexYacc 11.x documentation and source, the issue is that in version 11.x, the lexer file format changed. The `let` bindings for character classes are no longer supported in the same way. Instead, we should use inline character class definitions directly in the rules.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach - define the character classes inline without using `let` bindings, or use the `%{ ... %}` header block properly.\\n\\nActually, looking more carefully at FsLexYacc 11.x, the issue might be that the `let` bindings need to be in a separate section. In the newer FsLex format, the structure is:\\n1. Header in `{ }`\\n2. Character class definitions (using `let` or `%{` syntax)\\n3. Rules\\n\\nBut in version 11.x, the `let` bindings for character classes were removed. We need to use inline character classes directly.\\n\\nLet me rewrite the lexer to use inline character classes.\\n\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
